DAY 25 - PART B: LAB
Dynamic provisioning, persistence, static PVs, reclaim policy
Time: 90 minutes
==================================================================

cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week4


STEP 1: Look at kind's StorageClass
-----------------------------------
k get storageclass
k describe sc standard

Note: PROVISIONER rancher.io/local-path, RECLAIMPOLICY Delete,
VOLUMEBINDINGMODE WaitForFirstConsumer, "(default)".
k get pods -n local-path-storage      # the provisioner


STEP 2: Create a PVC
--------------------
vi pvc-data.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: data
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi

k apply -f pvc-data.yaml
k get pvc               # Pending
k describe pvc data | tail -3     # waiting for first consumer
k get pv                # nothing yet


STEP 3: Use it in a pod
-----------------------
vi pod-pvc.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: writer
spec:
  containers:
  - name: c
    image: busybox:1.36
    command: ["sh", "-c", "echo \"written at $(date) by $(hostname)\" >> /data/notes.txt; sleep 3600"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: data
      mountPath: /data
  volumes:
  - name: data
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: data

k apply -f pod-pvc.yaml
k get pvc,pv            # Bound; a PV appeared automatically
k get pod writer -o wide
k exec writer -- cat /data/notes.txt


STEP 4: Prove persistence
-------------------------
k delete pod writer
k apply -f pod-pvc.yaml
k exec writer -- cat /data/notes.txt       # 2 lines

Where is the data?
  k get pv -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.local.path}{.items[0].spec.hostPath.path}{"\n"}'
  docker exec <node-from-wide-output> ls /opt/local-path-provisioner/

Also notice the PV's nodeAffinity: local-path disks are tied to one
node, so the pod will always be scheduled there:
  k get pv -o yaml | grep -A8 nodeAffinity


STEP 5: Reclaim policy Delete
-----------------------------
PV=$(k get pvc data -o jsonpath='{.spec.volumeName}')
k delete pod writer
k delete pvc data
k get pv                  # gone: PV and data deleted

Lesson: with "Delete", deleting a PVC destroys the data.


STEP 6: A Retain StorageClass
-----------------------------
vi sc-retain.yaml

apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: local-retain
provisioner: rancher.io/local-path
reclaimPolicy: Retain
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer

k apply -f sc-retain.yaml
Edit pvc-data.yaml: add "storageClassName: local-retain" under spec.
k apply -f pvc-data.yaml && k apply -f pod-pvc.yaml
k get pvc,pv
k delete pod writer ; k delete pvc data
k get pv                  # STATUS Released: disk kept

A Released PV can't be re-bound automatically (it still references
the old claim). Admin options: copy the data, or remove claimRef:
  PV=$(k get pv -o jsonpath='{.items[?(@.spec.storageClassName=="local-retain")].metadata.name}')
  k patch pv $PV --type=json -p '[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/claimRef"}]'
  k get pv                # Available


STEP 7: Static provisioning (hostPath PV, lab only)
---------------------------------------------------
vi pv-static.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: static-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 2Gi
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: manual
  hostPath:
    path: /tmp/static-pv
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: static-claim
spec:
  storageClassName: manual
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi

k apply -f pv-static.yaml
k get pv,pvc       # static-claim Bound to static-pv (2Gi satisfies 1Gi)


